# Add a hipObject engine to NIXL's OBJ plugin: S3 over RDMA on ROCm.
#
# NIXL has exactly one S3-over-RDMA engine, src/plugins/obj/s3_accel/dell, and
# it is CUDA-only -- it links the CUDA Toolkit's cuObject client and is
# compiled only when `dependency('cuobjclient-13.x')` is found. On a ROCm image
# the object path therefore stops at plain HTTP, with the payload in the body,
# which is the one thing an RDMA fabric is there to avoid.
#
# This adds the ROCm counterpart, selected with `accelerated=true` and
# `type=hipobj`, built on ROCm/hipObject.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE SAME PROTOCOL
#
# It is not a parallel invention. rocm-ernic's `--backend s3` already serves
# the protocol cuObject v1.2.0 introduced and hipObject speaks on the client
# side: an ordinary HTTP/1.1 S3 request carrying an x-amz-rdma-token header
# that describes a registered client buffer, with the payload placed into that
# buffer by RDMA before the response headers come back. So the server half has
# been in this project all along; this is the client half, reached through the
# NIXL backend API like any other transport.
#
# WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE
#
#   src/plugins/obj/s3_accel/amd/rdma_interface.h  iHipObjS3RdmaClient
#   src/plugins/obj/s3_accel/amd/client.{h,cpp}    the S3 requests
#   src/plugins/obj/s3_accel/amd/engine_impl.{h,cpp}  the engine
#
# laid out beside s3_accel/dell/ and registered through the same
# objAccelEngineRegistrar, so obj_backend.cpp needs no change at all: the
# registry already dispatches on the "type" custom parameter.
#
# THREE PLACES IT DIFFERS FROM THE DELL ENGINE, EACH FOR A REASON
#
# 1. It reads the response header. Dell's engine ignores what comes back; this
#    one cannot. A server that does not implement the protocol answers a
#    perfectly valid HTTP 200, having moved nothing, and sets x-amz-rdma-reply
#    to 501 -- or omits it entirely. Either way the registered buffer still
#    holds whatever it held before, and reporting success would be reporting a
#    transfer that did not happen. statusFromRdmaReply() turns both into
#    NIXL_ERR_BACKEND with a message that says which.
#
#    Getting at that header took a detour: the AWS SDK's generated result types
#    expose only modelled headers, and GetObjectResult drops anything else. The
#    headers-received event handler is the one place the raw HttpResponse is
#    reachable, so client.cpp copies the value out there.
#
# 2. It synchronises the staging buffer. hipObject registers a host staging
#    buffer when the NIC cannot reach device memory directly -- no dmabuf, or
#    no peer-to-peer path -- and then the server's RDMA lands there instead.
#    Callers driving a transfer through hipObjGetRdmaToken() must stage to host
#    before a PUT and back to the device after a GET; hipObjBufSync() is a
#    no-op, reporting success, when the buffer was registered directly. The GET
#    side happens inside the completion callback, which is what keeps
#    checkXfer()'s NIXL_SUCCESS meaning "the data is where the caller asked for
#    it". Given how much of this project's history is dmabuf working or not
#    working, the staged path is worth supporting rather than assuming away.
#
# 3. It refuses a transfer that does not start at a registered base.
#    hipObjGetRdmaToken() looks the buffer up by exact pointer and mints the
#    token for [devPtr, devPtr + size) -- it passes offset 0 internally and has
#    no parameter for anything else, unlike hipObjGet()/hipObjPut(). A
#    descriptor pointing into the middle of a registration would yield a token
#    naming the base, and the server would RDMA to the wrong address. prepXfer()
#    returns NIXL_ERR_INVALID_PARAM and says why instead.
#
# THE BUILD SIDE
#
# hipobj_dep mirrors cuobj_dep, but discovered by path rather than pkg-config,
# because hipObject installs a CMake package and no .pc file. Empty
# -Dhipobj_path -- the default -- leaves the engine out exactly as a missing
# cuObject leaves the Dell one out, and the rest of the OBJ plugin is
# unaffected either way.
#
# Note this is an ENGINE inside the OBJ plugin, not a plugin of its own. The
# whole of src/plugins/obj is skipped unless the AWS SDK is present, which is
# why docker/scripts/build-aws-sdk.sh had to come first.
#
# TWO INTEROP SEAMS FOUND BY RUNNING IT
#
# Both were found against rocm-ernic's `--backend s3` server on the emulated
# cluster, and both are worth knowing before anyone reads the code and wonders.
#
# 1. The NIC has to be named. hipObject chooses its RDMA device by proximity to
#    the GPU, which is the wrong answer when the S3 endpoint is served by one
#    particular NIC: rocm-ernic's server reads the client buffer with
#    `dma->from_host(ctx, tok.rkey, tok.remote_addr, ...)`, resolving the rkey
#    in its own MR table -- "because one guest owns every registration this
#    emulator holds". An rkey minted on the other rail's device means nothing
#    to it, and the symptom is a 25-second stall then HTTP 500 "The RDMA read
#    from the client buffer did not complete." hipObject takes the NIC name
#    only through hipObjConfig_t, with no environment override, so the engine
#    exposes it as the `hipobj_nic` backend parameter and this patch adds
#    --obj_hipobj_nic to nixlbench to reach it. --obj_hipobj_gpu is its
#    companion.
#
# 2. x-amz-rdma-reply: 206 is not 200. hipObject's docs/interop.rst says the
#    only token-bearing form is "200:<88-hex>" and its parser enforces exactly
#    that. rocm-ernic answers a ranged GET with "206:<88-hex>" -- the correct
#    HTTP status for a Range request, and one hipObject's own public header
#    lists among the valid reply codes (200, 204, 206, 501). So a GET that
#    completed perfectly well comes back as hipObjInvalidValue. The engine
#    reads the leading status itself when hipObjParseRdmaReply refuses, and
#    says so in the log; it never uses the peer token, which is the only thing
#    the two specs actually disagree about.
#
#    This is a real disagreement between two AMD components, not a bug in
#    either one alone, and it should be settled upstream rather than papered
#    over in every client.
